--TEST--
`Core\Env::EOL`, `::OS` and `::VERSION` each answer one fact about the machine, so a case asserts the shape every platform's answer has to have rather than the answer one platform gives — `EOL` ends in a line feed and is one or two characters, `OS` is one of `Core\Env::Platform`'s six cases, and `VERSION` is three dot-separated pieces — spec § 15, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R6
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A constant is folded at the site that names it, so this case cannot print any
// of the three: two of them differ between the Windows leg and the WSL one, and
// the third changes on every release. What is the same everywhere is their
// *shape*, and each assertion below is written so that a wrong value on either
// platform fails rather than passing on one of them.

// `EOL` is `\r\n` or `\n` and nothing else -- asserted from both ends, because a
// constant that answered `\r` alone would still be one character and a constant
// that answered a whole sentence would still end in a line feed.
int $eol = Core\Str::length(Core\Env::EOL) as int;
if (Core\Str::endsWith(Core\Env::EOL, "\n") && $eol >= 1 && $eol <= 2) {
    echo "EOL: a line feed, at most one character before it\n";
} else {
    echo "EOL: something else\n";
}

// Nothing reads it: spec § 1 says the platform's line ending is never consulted
// when text is split, so a file written with `\r\n` and one written with `\n`
// have the same lines whatever this constant says. That is the claim, and it is
// asserted by agreement between the two members that split.
array<string> $split = Core\Str::lines("one\r\ntwo\nthree");
echo "lines across both terminators: ", Core\Arr::count($split) as string, "\n";

// `OS` is a case of the closed `Core\Env::Platform` enum, not `uname`'s free
// text. Counted over every case rather than compared against one, so the
// assertion says the same thing on every platform this suite runs on.
array<Core\Env::Platform> $families = [
    Core\Env::Platform::Windows,
    Core\Env::Platform::Darwin,
    Core\Env::Platform::Linux,
    Core\Env::Platform::BSD,
    Core\Env::Platform::Solaris,
    Core\Env::Platform::Unknown,
];
int $named = 0;
foreach ($families as Core\Env::Platform $family) {
    if ($family == Core\Env::OS) {
        $named = $named + 1;
    }
}
echo "OS is one of the families: ", $named as string, "\n";

// `VERSION` is three dot-separated pieces and every one of them is there: a
// constant that answered an empty string would split to one piece, and one that
// ended in a dot would split to three with an empty last.
array<string> $pieces = Core\Str::split(Core\Env::VERSION, ".");
int $filled = 0;
foreach ($pieces as string $piece) {
    if (!Core\Str::isEmpty($piece)) {
        $filled = $filled + 1;
    }
}
echo "VERSION pieces: ", Core\Arr::count($pieces) as string,
     ", none of them empty: ", $filled as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
EOL: a line feed, at most one character before it
lines across both terminators: 3
OS is one of the families: 1
VERSION pieces: 3, none of them empty: 3
